<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greenland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalaallit Nunaat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Greenland is the world's largest island, lying between the North Atlantic and Arctic oceans, and an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Norse settlers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a> founded colonies there around 985, which accepted Norwegian rule in 1261 but died out in the 15th century. Danish-Norwegian colonisation resumed in 1721; Greenland gained home rule in 1979 and self-government in 2009. Most of its people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greenland.jpg" alt="Greenland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeffrey Connell (IceKarma) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greenland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Greenland is the world's largest non-continental island, lying between latitudes 59° and 83°N, with over 80% of its land north of the Arctic Circle. Its area is 2,166,086 square kilometres, of which the ice sheet covers about 81%. The highest point is Gunnbjørn Fjeld, at 3,700 metres, but most of the island lies below 1,500 metres. Nearly all of its people live along the fjords of the south-west, where the climate is relatively mild.</p><p>The early Norse settlers gave the island its name. The sagas say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, named the land Grœnland in the hope that a pleasant name would attract settlers. In the Greenlandic language it is Kalaallit Nunaat, land of the Kalaallit, the principal group of Greenlandic Inuit in the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Successive Palaeo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> cultures, known mainly from archaeology, lived on the island. The first entry is thought to have been about 2500 BCE. The Saqqaq culture inhabited the south and west from about 2500 to 800 BCE, with most finds around Disko Bay, and the Independence I culture lived in the north from 2400 to 1300 BCE. About 800 BCE the Saqqaq culture disappeared, and the Early Dorset and Independence II cultures emerged. The Thule people entered later from the north.</p><p>From the late 10th century Icelanders and Norwegians settled the west coast, in a contingent of 14 boats led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>. They formed the Eastern, Western and Middle Settlements on fjords near the south-western tip. The Norse Greenlanders submitted to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1261. Settlements such as Brattahlíð lasted for centuries but vanished in the 15th century, perhaps at the onset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a>. Dano-Norwegian explorers returned in the 17th century, found the earlier settlement extinct and re-established a Scandinavian presence.</p><p>Greenland passed from the Norwegian to the Danish crown when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> separated in 1814. Denmark integrated it into the Danish Realm in 1952, ending its colonial status, and granted home rule in 1979. The Self-Government Act, passed after a referendum in 2008, transferred further areas of government to the Naalakkersuisut, the Greenlandic government, and made Greenlandic the sole official language in 2009.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuuk">Nuuk</a> as its capital and largest city. A population of 56,583 was counted in 2022, most of them Greenlandic Inuit, with a Danish minority. Its parliament is the Inatsisartut. Greenlandic culture blends Inuit and Scandinavian traditions, among them the carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> figures called tupilak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greenland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Denmark and Norway thus remained under one king of the house of Oldenburg, together with Iceland, the Faroe Islands and the Norse claims to <b>Greenland</b>, while the king also ruled the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The union of crowns with Norway from 1380 to 1814 made Danish the written language of Norway, and Iceland, the Faroe Islands and <b>Greenland</b> stayed under the Danish crown after Norway’s departure, so that Danish administration and language shaped the whole North Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norwegians settled the Faroe Islands, Iceland, Shetland, Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern Britain, and from Iceland reached <b>Greenland</b> and, around 1000, Newfoundland, where the Norse site at L'Anse aux Meadows has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…to later Czech and Slovak national histories, and it gave its name to the Moravian Church: Brethren refugees from Moravia settled at Herrnhut in Saxony in 1722 under Count Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf, and their renewed church sent missionaries to the Caribbean, <b>Greenland</b>, North America and southern Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…area: to England and Ireland, where they founded kingdoms and towns such as York and Dublin; to Normandy; to the Faroe Islands, Iceland, settled from around 870, and <b>Greenland</b>; along the rivers of Russia and Ukraine to Constantinople and the Caspian Sea; and briefly to Newfoundland around 1000.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse speakers also settled in Normandy, whose name means “land of the Northmen”, in the Faroe Islands, in Iceland, and in <b>Greenland</b>, where Erik the Red founded settlements around 985; around 1000 Norse sailors reached North America, at L’Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
